<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title>OpenELP DB9CR APT repository</title>
@@HREFLANG_LINKS@@
<style>
  body { font-family: system-ui, sans-serif; max-width: 46rem; margin: 2rem auto; padding: 0 1rem; line-height: 1.5; }
  pre { background: #f0f0f0; padding: 0.75rem 1rem; overflow-x: auto; border-radius: 4px; }
  code { background: #f0f0f0; padding: 0.1rem 0.3rem; border-radius: 3px; }
  pre code { background: none; padding: 0; }
  table { border-collapse: collapse; margin: 1rem 0; }
  th, td { border: 1px solid #ccc; padding: 0.3rem 0.6rem; text-align: left; }
  .note { color: #555; font-size: 0.95em; }
  .langs { font-size: 0.85em; line-height: 1.8; border-bottom: 1px solid #ccc; padding-bottom: 0.5rem; }
  .langs a, .langs strong { white-space: nowrap; margin-right: 0.6em; }
  .ai-notice { background: #fff4ce; border: 1px solid #e0c060; border-radius: 4px; padding: 0.5rem 0.75rem; }
</style>
</head>
<body>
@@LANG_SWITCHER@@
<h1>OpenELP DB9CR APT repository</h1>
@@AI_NOTICE@@
<p>Packages of <a href="https://github.com/@@REPO_SLUG@@">OpenELP</a>, the
Open Source EchoLink Proxy, for Raspberry Pi OS and Debian, in two
components &mdash; pick one:</p>
<ul>
<li><code>main</code>: daily development builds from the latest source of
this fork, not stable releases.</li>
<li><code>release</code>: the official upstream OpenELP releases (0.9.3 and
newer), built from the unmodified upstream release source with the same
build settings as the daily packages. Every release is kept;
<code>apt upgrade</code> moves to each new upstream release.</li>
</ul>
<p>See the <a href="https://github.com/@@REPO_SLUG@@">source repository</a>
for details.</p>

<h2>Upstream &amp; local changes</h2>
<p>This is a daily-synced fork of
<a href="https://github.com/cottsay/openelp">cottsay/openelp</a>, the
original OpenELP project, with local packaging (this APT repository) and
possibly further changes layered on top. To see exactly what is different
from upstream at any point in time, compare live on GitHub:
<a href="https://github.com/cottsay/openelp/compare/main...criede:openelp:main">upstream main&nbsp;&hellip;&nbsp;this fork's main</a>.</p>

<h2>Quickstart</h2>
<p>Run on the target system (detects the right codename/architecture automatically):</p>
<pre><code># Import the public signing key
curl -fsSL https://criede.github.io/openelp/pubkey.gpg | \
  sudo gpg --dearmor -o /usr/share/keyrings/openelp-db9cr.gpg

# Add the repository source. Choose the component:
#   main     daily development builds
#   release  official upstream releases (0.9.3 and newer)
component=release
codename=$(sed -n 's/^VERSION_CODENAME=//p' /etc/os-release | tr -d '"')
arch=$(dpkg --print-architecture)
echo "deb [signed-by=/usr/share/keyrings/openelp-db9cr.gpg arch=${arch}] https://criede.github.io/openelp ${codename} ${component}" | \
  sudo tee /etc/apt/sources.list.d/openelp-db9cr.list

sudo apt update
sudo apt install openelp

# Mandatory before the first start: set a password (and optionally the
# port, bind addresses, registration and callsign filters).
sudo editor /etc/openelp/ELProxy.conf

# Enable the proxy at boot and start it now.
sudo systemctl enable --now openelp.service

# Check service state and follow its log.
systemctl status openelp.service
journalctl -u openelp.service -f</code></pre>
<p class="note"><strong>Required:</strong> The proxy refuses to start
while <code>Password</code> in <code>/etc/openelp/ELProxy.conf</code> is
still <code>notset</code>. Choose a password of your own; set it to
<code>PUBLIC</code> only if you want to run a public proxy (together with a
unique <code>RegistrationName</code>).</p>
<p class="note">If <code>apt update</code> reports a 404 for this source, your
system's codename isn't built yet (see the table below) &mdash; edit the
codename in <code>/etc/apt/sources.list.d/openelp-db9cr.list</code> to one of
the supported ones instead. Afterwards, a regular <code>sudo apt upgrade</code>
picks up new daily builds (<code>main</code>) or new upstream releases
(<code>release</code>) automatically and restarts a running proxy.</p>
<p class="note">Do not enable both components at once: apt always installs
whichever version is higher, which is usually a daily build. To switch an
existing installation from <code>main</code> to <code>release</code>, change
the component in <code>/etc/apt/sources.list.d/openelp-db9cr.list</code>, run
<code>sudo apt update</code>, then install the release version explicitly
(usually a downgrade), e.g.
<code>sudo apt install openelp=0.9.3-1~trixie</code>.</p>

<h2>Network and firewall</h2>
<p>EchoLink clients connect to the proxy over TCP on the configured
<code>Port</code> (default <code>8100</code>). The proxy itself must be
reachable from the Internet on UDP ports <code>5198</code> and
<code>5199</code> (forward them on your router if the proxy is behind NAT)
and needs outgoing access to TCP port <code>5200</code> and UDP ports
<code>5198</code>/<code>5199</code>. Allow only your own clients to reach
the proxy port unless you run a public proxy.</p>

<h2>Using the proxy with SvxLink</h2>
<p>In SvxLink's EchoLink module configuration
(<code>/etc/svxlink/svxlink.d/ModuleEchoLink.conf</code>), point it to the
proxy and restart SvxLink:</p>
<pre><code>PROXY_SERVER=192.0.2.10    # address of the OpenELP host
PROXY_PORT=8100
PROXY_PASSWORD=your-password</code></pre>
<p class="note">The official EchoLink PC client is configured the same way
under <em>Setup &rarr; Connections &rarr; Proxy</em>.</p>

<h2>Applying configuration changes</h2>
<p>OpenELP reads its configuration only at startup. After editing
<code>/etc/openelp/ELProxy.conf</code>, restart the service (this drops
active client connections):</p>
<pre><code>sudo systemctl restart openelp.service</code></pre>
<p class="note">Local changes to <code>/etc/openelp/ELProxy.conf</code> are
kept on upgrades (it is a dpkg configuration file). The file is readable by
root only, since it contains the proxy password.</p>

<h2>Installing an older version</h2>
<p>Recent versions stay available in the pool for a while after being
superseded (daily for a week, weekly for a month, monthly for a year &mdash;
see "Retention" in
<a href="https://github.com/@@REPO_SLUG@@/blob/main/.github/UPSTREAM-SYNC.MD">UPSTREAM-SYNC.MD</a>),
so a bad daily build can usually be rolled back. Upstream release packages
(<code>release</code> component) are never removed:</p>
<pre><code>apt list -a openelp   # lists every version still in the repository
sudo apt install openelp=0.9.4~dev+daily20261108.12.1.g0123456789ab-1~trixie
sudo apt-mark hold openelp   # optional: stop "apt upgrade" pulling it forward again
# sudo apt-mark unhold openelp   # later, to resume automatic updates</code></pre>

<h2>Packages</h2>
<table>
<tr><th>Package</th><th>Contains</th></tr>
<tr><td><code>openelp</code></td><td>The <code>openelpd</code> proxy daemon and its library, the sample configuration <code>/etc/openelp/ELProxy.conf</code>, a systemd service (<code>openelp.service</code>, not enabled automatically) and the manual page. Built against the system PCRE2 library, with the bundled MD5 implementation (no OpenSSL dependency) and syslog logging. Development headers are not included.</td></tr>
@@EXTRA_PACKAGE_ROWS@@</table>

<h2>Supported systems</h2>
<table>
<tr><th>Codename</th><th>Matches</th><th>Architectures: <code>main</code> (daily)</th><th>Architectures: <code>release</code></th></tr>
@@SUITE_ROWS@@</table>
<p class="note"><strong>32-bit systems:</strong> <code>armhf</code> packages
are built against Raspbian (ARMv6+VFP), the base of 32-bit Raspberry Pi OS,
so they run on every Raspberry Pi model including the Pi&nbsp;1, Zero and
Zero&nbsp;W, and also install on Debian <code>armhf</code> (both are tested).
<code>i386</code> (32-bit PCs) is built for Debian&nbsp;12 only: Debian&nbsp;13
no longer supports installing i386 systems. The 32-bit packages contain the
same features as the 64-bit ones.</p>

<h2>Signing key</h2>
<p><a href="pubkey.gpg">pubkey.gpg</a> &mdash; imported automatically by the
quickstart commands above.</p>

<h2>Browse repository files</h2>
<p><a href="dists/">dists/</a> (per-suite indices, Release files, Packages
lists) &middot; <a href="pool/">pool/</a> (the actual <code>.deb</code>
files) &middot; <a href="https://github.com/@@REPO_SLUG@@">source
repository</a> on GitHub.</p>
</body>
</html>
